<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Hannington</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Hannington was an English Anglican missionary and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">Equatorial Africa</a>. In 1885 he tried to reach Buganda by the eastern route through Busoga, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Baganda</a> feared as the direction of invasion. On the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a> he was seized and killed in Busoga in October 1885, an event that preceded the persecution of Christians in Buganda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-hannington.jpg" alt="James Hannington" width="298" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael, Charles D · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bishop_Hannington.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
